    {
      "id": "2fb07645-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 0,
      "content": "This by-major agreement displays the lower-division courses required in the major: Cal Poly Humboldt courses on the left, and approved (articulated) transfer courses on the right. See the Office of Admissions website for current admission requirements. Completion of all coursework listed in this agreement is not necessarily mandatory prior to admission. Check the University Catalog for a full listing of degree and major requirements. Students may satisfy lower division GE requirements with the CSU General Education (CSU-GE) Breadth pattern, the Intersegmental General Education Transfer Curriculum (IGETC), or by earning an Associate Degree for Transfer (AA-T/AS-T). The articulation agreement below is subject to periodic revision. Consult a counselor every semester to obtain current information about possible changes in the agreement. Additional Resources earn college credit for examinations (AP, IB, CLEP, &amp; more) learn about transfer credit at Cal Poly Humboldt explore living &amp; learning at Cal Poly Humboldt Questions? Contact articulation@humboldt.edu",
      "contentHtml": "<p>This by-major agreement displays the lower-division courses required in the major: Cal Poly Humboldt courses on the left, and approved (articulated) transfer courses on the right. See the <a href=\"https://admissions.humboldt.edu/node/6\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Office of Admissions website</a> for current admission requirements. Completion of all coursework listed in this agreement is not necessarily mandatory prior to admission. Check the <a href=\"https://catalog.humboldt.edu\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">University Catalog</a> for a full listing of <a href=\"https://catalog.humboldt.edu/content.php?catoid=12&amp;navoid=2009\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">degree</a><a href=\"https://catalog.humboldt.edu/content.php?catoid=8&amp;navoid=1623\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"> </a>and <a href=\"https://catalog.humboldt.edu/content.php?catoid=12&amp;navoid=2009#majors\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">major</a> requirements. Students may satisfy lower division GE requirements with the CSU General Education (CSU-GE) Breadth pattern, the Intersegmental General Education Transfer Curriculum (IGETC), or by earning an Associate Degree for Transfer (AA-T/AS-T). The articulation agreement below is subject to periodic revision. Consult a counselor every semester to obtain current information about possible changes in the agreement.</p><p>Additional Resources</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://catalog.humboldt.edu/content.php?catoid=12&amp;navoid=1939#credit-by-exam\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">earn college credit for examinations</a><a href=\"https://registrar.humboldt.edu/sites/default/files/pdf/catalog/general-sections/exams.pdf\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"> </a>(AP, IB, CLEP, &amp; more)</li><li>learn about <a href=\"https://registrar.humboldt.edu/transfer-credit\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">transfer credit at Cal Poly Humboldt</a></li><li>explore <a href=\"http://humboldt.edu/why\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">living &amp; learning at Cal Poly Humboldt </a></li></ul><p><br></p><p>Questions? Contact <a href=\"mailto:articulation@humboldt.edu\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">articulation@humboldt.edu</a></p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "dfa07645-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralTitle",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 1,
      "content": "Minimum grade of c- in major courses",
      "contentHtml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "b3fd6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 2,
      "content": "Students must earn a minimum grade of C- in all courses taken to fulfill the major requirements; and must earn a minimum overall GPA of 2.0 in the major.",
      "contentHtml": "Students must earn a minimum grade of C- in all courses taken to fulfill the major requirements; and must earn a minimum overall GPA of 2.0 in the major."
    },
    {
      "id": "1dfd6f45-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralTitle",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 3,
      "content": "Child Development & Family Relations Major information",
      "contentHtml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "12e57645-6887-ef11-8474-000d3a37e3b6",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 4,
      "content": "Students considering this major are strongly urged to review the University Catalog for further information about this major, the concentrations, emphases and other upper division course options. Many of the areas below are primarily composed of upper division coursework not reflected here on ASSIST, only lower division courses are published.Humboldt’s program is unique among the child development programs in that:core courses (which all students take) give cohesive and comprehensive attention to children’s development and socialization;an on-campus practicum is required;additional practicums with children or families are available in the emphasis areas;coursework beyond the core is based on the student’s own identified special interests.In core courses students learn basic principles and theories of child development as well as practices that support children and families. Students select one of the following three concentrations: Child and Family Services, Teaching or Specialized Studies. Within the first two concentrations, students also select an emphasis. The Specialized Studies concentration is individually designed between student and advisor.Important note for transfer students: Students may not use the same transferable course for both CD 253 and CD 255. Please select another course to meet the remaining courses required.",
      "contentHtml": "<p>Students considering this major are strongly urged to review the <a href=\"https://catalog.humboldt.edu/preview_program.php?catoid=14&amp;poid=8293&amp;returnto=2259\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">University Catalog</a> for further information about this major, the concentrations, emphases and other upper division course options. Many of the areas below are primarily composed of upper division coursework not reflected here on ASSIST, only lower division courses are published.Humboldt’s program is unique among the child development programs in that:core courses (which all students take) give cohesive and comprehensive attention to children’s development and socialization;an on-campus practicum is required;additional practicums with children or families are available in the emphasis areas;coursework beyond the core is based on the student’s own identified special interests.In core courses students learn basic principles and theories of child development as well as practices that support children and families. Students select one of the following three concentrations: Child and Family Services, Teaching or Specialized Studies. Within the first two concentrations, students also select an emphasis. The Specialized Studies concentration is individually designed between student and advisor.Important note for transfer students: Students may not use the same transferable course for both CD 253 and CD 255. Please select another course to meet the remaining courses required.</p>"
    },
    {
